Chapter: SelectorNoField

 A dot that opens a selector must be followed by a field name, and saying
 so is the whole point of this test. Until this was fixed the
 parser returned ExprOk here WITHOUT consuming the dot, so the statement
 loop met the same token forever: the heap climbed, the stack sank a
 gigabyte, and the compile double-faulted with no diagnostic at all.

 The dot is inside an act block deliberately, and the test is worthless
 anywhere else. At the top level the def parser takes the error node and
 moves on, so a stray dot there merely reports CDX2000 and this test would
 pass against the very compiler it exists to catch. The act statement loop
 is the caller that loops on the state it is handed back.

 The real-world trigger was an act block left unclosed, which makes that
 same loop read the prose beneath it as code and meet the period ending an
 English sentence. This is that dot, reduced.

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
   print-line "codex"
   .
  end
